  "privacyTitle": "Privacy",
  "privacyOfflineTitle": "Nothing leaves your phone",
  "privacyOffline": "OpenFinances has no account, no servers, no ads and no analytics. It doesn't even have permission to use the internet, so Android won't let it go online",
  "privacyDataTitle": "Your data",
  "privacyData": "Your expenses, income, budgets, debts and settings stay only in the app's storage on this phone, unless you turn on Android backup. Uninstalling the app deletes them",
  "privacyBackupsTitle": "Backups",
  "privacyBackups": "A backup is made only when you ask for one: a file saved where you choose. Where it goes from there is up to you. Android's own backup to your Google account is off unless you tick Allow Android to back up my data in Settings, under Data. Ticked, Android copies the app's data only when it encrypts it with your screen lock, so Google can't read it. Unticked, Android's own phone-to-phone transfer leaves the data out too, though some phone makers' transfer tools may still copy it",
  "privacyBankTitle": "Bank messages and notification access",
  "privacyBank": "Off unless you turn it on. Then Android asks you to allow notification access, so the app can read the notifications your messaging app shows for new texts and suggest entries for bank transactions. Only messages recognized as bank transactions are kept; every other notification is ignored. Nothing is sent anywhere",
  "privacyUpdatesTitle": "Update check",
  "privacyUpdates": "Only when you tap Check for updates in About, Google Play (not this app) looks for a newer version; Google receives the app's version and device details",
  "privacyLockTitle": "Fingerprint lock",
  "privacyLock": "Your fingerprint is checked by Android itself; the app never sees it",
  "privacyRatingTitle": "Rating the app",
  "privacyRating": "Now and then, after you save entries, Google Play may ask you to rate the app. The app only counts saves on this phone. A rating goes to Google Play, never to the app",
